From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] core: hold a new shell back until it's released

A restart of the shell left the launcher, the bar and the lock client
gone for about a second while the new one loaded, and a chord said then
waited for it. A shell started with QS_HELD naming a file now loads
showing none of its windows and serving none of its sockets, writes
"ready" to that file once its configuration has loaded, and shows each
window and serves each socket as it was asked to once SIGUSR1 releases
it. So a new shell can load beside the one it replaces, which goes on
drawing and answering until it's ended.

A shell started held also asks polkit to take its agent again, every
250 ms for 10 s, since the session's agent stays the old shell's until
that one ends, and polkit refuses a second. Started without QS_HELD, a
shell is as it was.

---
diff -ru a/src/core/reload.cpp c/src/core/reload.cpp
--- a/src/core/reload.cpp	2026-11-13 21:18:50.567210897 -0500
+++ c/src/core/reload.cpp	2026-11-13 21:19:59.470628585 -0500
@@ -1,8 +1,15 @@
 #include "reload.hpp"
 
+#include <functional>
+#include <utility>
+
 #include <qcontainerfwd.h>
+#include <qlist.h>
 #include <qobject.h>
+#include <qpair.h>
+#include <qpointer.h>
 #include <qqmllist.h>
+#include <qtenvironmentvariables.h>
 #include <qtimer.h>
 
 #include "generation.hpp"
@@ -144,3 +151,36 @@
 	this->isPostReload = true;
 	this->onPostReload();
 }
+
+namespace {
+
+bool sHeld = !qEnvironmentVariableIsEmpty("QS_HELD");
+
+QList<QPair<QPointer<QObject>, std::function<void()>>>& waitingOnRelease() {
+	static QList<QPair<QPointer<QObject>, std::function<void()>>> waiting;
+	return waiting;
+}
+
+} // namespace
+
+bool HeldUntilReleased::held() { return sHeld; }
+
+void HeldUntilReleased::onRelease(QObject* context, std::function<void()> run) {
+	if (!sHeld) {
+		run();
+		return;
+	}
+
+	waitingOnRelease().append({context, std::move(run)});
+}
+
+void HeldUntilReleased::release() {
+	if (!sHeld) return;
+	sHeld = false;
+
+	// Taken first, as what runs may ask for more, which now runs at once.
+	auto waiting = std::exchange(waitingOnRelease(), {});
+	for (auto& [context, run]: waiting) {
+		if (context) run();
+	}
+}
